# Baseline for tests\autotest\run_extractor_version_guard.ps1
#
# <DRAGLINT_EXTRACTOR_VERSION>|<sha256 of the extraction surface>
#
# The extraction surface is src\parser, src\preprocess, src\index and
# DRagLint.Core.Indexer.pas -- the code that decides WHAT gets extracted from a
# byte sequence. Change any of it and the guard fails until
# DRAGLINT_EXTRACTOR_VERSION is bumped, because every existing index would
# otherwise keep its old parses silently.
#
# To update: bump the constant in src\core\DRagLint.Core.Model.pas, re-run the
# guard, paste the line it prints below. Expect one full re-parse per index --
# that is the point of the bump.
#
# If a change genuinely cannot affect extraction (a comment, a rename with no
# behavioural effect), update the hash WITHOUT bumping the version and say why
# here.
#
# DEFER-UNTIL|<yyyy-MM-dd>|<hash>|<reason>
#   Postpones a bump that IS genuinely needed, deliberately and visibly. It is
#   pinned to one hash, so any further extractor change stops it applying and
#   the guard fails again at once; and it expires on its date, so the debt
#   cannot be forgotten. It is louder than a bump, not quieter -- every run
#   prints what is stale and when it falls due. Use it only when the owner has
#   asked for the fix now and the re-parse later.
# 2026-08-19: bumped 1.4.0-alpha -> 1.6.0-alpha for const signatures (a
#   declConst now emits its type and value into `signature`, which was empty
#   for every const in every index). This bump also DISCHARGES the
#   DEFER-UNTIL|2026-08-21 above: the constructor/destructor extraction fix it
#   was postponing rides the same re-parse, so the debt is settled two days
#   early rather than expiring. The owner's library rebuild planned for
#   2026-08-21 now covers both.
# 2026-08-24: hash updated WITHOUT a version bump, per the rule above.
#   src\index\DRagLint.Index.ManifestWrite.pas was added -- a manifest WRITER
#   for the new `register-project` verb. It lives under src\index because that
#   is where DRagLint.Index.Manifest (its read half) lives, so it falls inside
#   the hashed surface, and the guard correctly noticed.
#
#   It CANNOT affect extraction: it appends a section to drag-lint.json and is
#   never called during a walk. Nothing it does changes what the parser sees in
#   a byte sequence, so no stored parse becomes stale. Bumping would have cost
#   a ~5-hour re-parse of every index on the box to say nothing new.
#
#   The judgement being made explicitly, because the failure mode of getting
#   this wrong is SILENT: if a future edit to this file ever influenced what is
#   indexed, the version MUST move.
# 2026-08-26: hash updated WITHOUT a version bump, per the rule above.
#   `document --project` regenerated the managed DocInsight blocks across the
#   whole repo (16210c6), and 23 files of the extraction surface carry them:
#   src\parser, src\preprocess, src\index and Core.Indexer.pas, 658 insertions
#   and 563 deletions between them.
#
#   EVERY ONE of those lines is a `///` doc comment. Verified mechanically, not
#   by eye -- `git diff 9f78db3..HEAD -U0` over exactly the four hashed roots,
#   with `///` and blank lines filtered out, returns NOTHING. No declaration
#   moved, no code changed.
#
#   A doc comment cannot change what the parser sees in a byte sequence, so no
#   stored parse becomes stale. Bumping would have re-parsed every index on the
#   box -- ~7,000 library files plus every project -- to say nothing new, which
#   is the exact cost the DRAGLINT_VERSION/DRAGLINT_EXTRACTOR_VERSION split was
#   made to avoid.
#
#   Same explicit judgement as the 2026-08-24 entry, and the same warning: the
#   failure mode of getting this wrong is SILENT. If a future edit to these
#   files ever influences what is indexed, the version MUST move.
# 2026-08-26 (2nd): hash updated WITHOUT a version bump. CODE changed this time,
#   not just comments, so the reasoning is spelled out rather than asserted.
#
#   WHAT CHANGED: TClosureResolver.ExtractUses (src\index\DRagLint.Index.Closure.pas)
#   and the duplicate parser in DRagLint.Index.Reconcile.pas now collapse
#   whitespace around the dot of a qualified unit name before tokenising. A
#   column-aligned entry -- `, DRagLint.Doc        .Batch`, of which
#   DRagLint.CLI.pas alone has 64 -- previously yielded two junk tokens and never
#   the real unit name.
#
#   WHY NO BUMP. The rule this file applies is "does any STORED PARSE become
#   stale?", and nothing here changes what is extracted FROM a file: the parser,
#   the preprocessor and the extractors are untouched. What can change is the
#   SET of files a project closure reaches. For this repo it did not -- closure
#   108 = indexed 108, measured before AND after the fix -- because a .dproj
#   member list already covered those units.
#
#   Where it COULD differ is a project with a unit reachable ONLY through an
#   aligned uses entry and NOT listed as a project member. That closure now
#   grows by one file. That file is then indexed and parsed FRESH on the next
#   incremental run; no previously stored parse is invalidated, which is exactly
#   the failure mode the fingerprint exists to prevent. A bump would re-parse
#   ~7,000 library files plus every project index to produce byte-identical
#   symbols for every file that was already there.
#
#   THE RESIDUAL RISK, stated so it is not discovered later: if a future reader
#   concludes that "which files are in the closure" IS part of extraction, then
#   this entry is the one to revisit. The judgement here is that it is not,
#   because the fingerprint guards the CONTENT of a parse, not the membership of
#   a file set -- and membership is re-derived on every index run, while a parse
#   is not.
# 2026-08-26 (3rd): same run, same reasoning. DRagLint.Index.Reconcile.pas
#   changed again -- MakeRelPath now emits a proper relative path via
#   ExtractRelativePath instead of falling back to an ABSOLUTE one, and
#   EditDpr splices before the last clause entry so the main unit stays last.
#   Both are about what --apply WRITES into a .dpr/.dproj. Neither the parser,
#   the preprocessor, nor any extractor is touched, and no stored parse can
#   become stale, so the version does not move.
# 2026-08-26 (4th): a REAL BUMP, 1.7.0-alpha -> 1.8.0-alpha. src\parser changed
#   what it produces, so every stored parse IS stale and a full re-parse is the
#   point, not a cost to be argued away.
#
#   WHAT CHANGED: a generic type instantiation emitted NO ref. `TList<T>` and
#   `TDictionary<K,V>` produced nothing for the base type name, a DECLARATION
#   emitted nothing at all (not even its type arguments), and a construction
#   site `TList<T>.Create` also lost its `Create` member-access. The comment at
#   EmitTypeUseReference had GUESSED the node shape -- it claimed
#   `(typeref (identifier) (declTypeArgs ...))`; the grammar has no such node,
#   so the whole typeref fell to the `else Exit`. The real shapes, dumped from
#   the tree, are typerefTpl (declarations) and exprTpl (expressions).
#
#   THE MEASUREMENT: 3,878 generic instantiation sites in this repo's own src#   produced 16 refs, and no ref anywhere in any index carried a '<' -- so the
#   names were not merely mangled, they were absent. Consumers affected:
#   query type-usage, unused-unit-in-uses, the call graph (every generic
#   construction was an invisible call site), and unused-public-symbol.
#
#   Every index on the box must be re-parsed to pick the new refs up. Until it
#   is, a query about a generic type answers confidently with fewer results --
#   which is the failure this fingerprint exists to make impossible to ignore.
#
#   Pinned by tests\autotest\run_generic_typeref_refs.ps1.
# 2026-08-26: hash updated WITHOUT a version bump, per the rule above.
#   src\index\DRagLint.Index.Closure.pas gained 29 lines: the .dpr branch of
#   Resolve now reads a sibling .dproj for its DCC_UnitSearchPath. It is the
#   ONLY surface file changed since 0fdee4a (verified with git diff over the
#   four hashed roots).
#
#   It CANNOT affect extraction. The version exists because the incremental skip
#   compares path+mtime+sha -- FILE identity -- and so cannot tell that a newer
#   engine would parse an UNCHANGED file differently. This change does not touch
#   what the parser produces from any byte sequence; it changes WHICH FILES the
#   closure walk reaches, by adding search paths. Every file it newly reaches is
#   a new PATH, so the skip indexes it fresh -- there is no stored parse for it
#   to keep. And the change only ADDS paths, so no file leaves the closure with
#   a stale row behind it.
#
#   Same shape as the 2026-08-24 entry: a src\index file inside the hashed
#   surface that plays no part in a walk's extraction.

# 2026-08-26 (2nd): hash updated WITHOUT a version bump -- `document --project`.
#   A full autodoc pass rewrote managed DocInsight blocks across 40 files, seven
#   of them inside the hashed surface (Index.Closure, Index.Coverage, Index.Plan,
#   Index.Reconcile, Parser.DFM, Parser.SpringDI, Preprocess.Types).
#
#   PROVEN comment-only, not asserted: `git diff -U0` over exactly the four
#   hashed roots, with every added/removed line that is neither blank nor a
#   `///` line collected -> ZERO. The same check over all 40 changed files also
#   returns zero. A `///` comment cannot change a parse, so no stored parse in
#   any index becomes stale and a re-parse would buy nothing.
#
#   The pass reached a FIXED POINT before this was recorded: run, reindex, run
#   again -> "1100 public decl(s), nothing to document", with the working diff
#   byte-identical to the first run. Autodoc has a documented non-idempotency
#   history, so a clean first run alone would not have been evidence.

# 2026-08-27: hash updated WITHOUT a version bump, per the rule above.
#   ONE file in the hashed surface changed -- src\index\DRagLint.Index.Manifest.pas,
#   verified with `git diff --stat` over exactly the four hashed roots: 4
#   insertions, 73 deletions, nothing else touched.
#
#   WHAT CHANGED: it is a MOVE, not a rewrite. The body of TManifestIO.Save --
#   Format(2), TEncoding.UTF8.GetBytes, temp file plus MoveFileEx swap -- moved
#   verbatim into the new src\core\DRagLint.Core.JsonFile.WriteJsonFileAtomic,
#   and Save now calls it. Same algorithm, same bytes on disk, different unit.
#
#   WHY IT MOVED: the IDE plugin needed to share it. Three copies in the
#   plugins options pages wrote Root.ToJSON through TEncoding.UTF8 instead --
#   minified, with a BOM -- and pressing OK in Tools > Options collapsed the
#   313-line drag-lint.json to a single 5 KB line. src\core is where it belongs:
#   src\index is inside THIS hashed surface, and the plugins design-time
#   package does not even compile against src\index (T64_lint_options_compile
#   went red proving it).
#
#   WHY NO BUMP. The question this file asks is "does any STORED PARSE become
#   stale?". This code serialises the CONFIG FILE. It never runs during a walk,
#   it reads no source, and it cannot change what the parser sees in any byte
#   sequence. Bumping would re-parse ~7,000 library files plus every project
#   index to produce byte-identical symbols -- exactly the cost the
#   DRAGLINT_VERSION / DRAGLINT_EXTRACTOR_VERSION split exists to avoid.
#
#   Same shape as the 2026-08-24 entry (a manifest WRITER under src\index), and
#   the same warning: the failure mode of getting this wrong is SILENT. If a
#   future edit to this file ever influences what is indexed, the version MUST
#   move.
#
#   Pinned by `drag-lint selftest manifest-save-atomic`, which now asserts the
#   written file carries no BOM and is not on one line -- both assertions were
#   run against a deliberately regressed writer and each failed on its own
#   defect before the fix was restored.

# 2026-08-28: hash updated WITHOUT a version bump, per the rule above.
#   ONE new file in the hashed surface: src\index\DRagLint.Index.Freshness.pas.
#   Nothing else under the four roots changed (the session also touched
#   Core.Model, Core.Interfaces, Storage.SQLite, CLI, Analysis.DataFlow and
#   Flow.Lattices -- none of which is hashed).
#
#   WHAT IT IS: the freshness sweep for the 2026-08-13 owner ruling. It reads
#   files.path / files.mtime_unix out of an EXISTING index and stats the disk,
#   to print a stderr note when the index no longer describes the source.
#
#   WHY NO BUMP. The question is "does any STORED PARSE become stale?". This
#   code reads an index; it never writes one. It sees no source bytes, it holds
#   no parser, and -- unlike the 2026-08-26 Closure entries, which had to argue
#   about closure MEMBERSHIP -- it plays no part in deciding which files are
#   reached either. There is no mechanism by which it can change what is
#   extracted from any byte sequence.
#
#   "IT NEVER RUNS DURING A WALK" WAS VERIFIED, NOT ASSERTED, because that is
#   the only load-bearing claim here:
#     - statically: NoteIndexFreshnessOnce has exactly three call sites --
#       OpenReadOnlyStore, DoLintAll, DoLintProject. DoIndex and BuildPlanItem
#       reach none of them (grep count 0); the index path constructs its own
#       TSQLiteSymbolStore directly.
#     - empirically: indexed a one-unit fixture, modified it so the DB was
#       genuinely stale, re-ran `index` -- NO note. If the probe were reachable
#       from a walk it would have fired there.
#
#   Same shape as the 2026-08-24 entry (ManifestWrite.pas) and the 2026-08-27
#   entry (JsonFile move): a src\index file inside the hashed surface that takes
#   no part in extraction. Bumping would re-parse ~7,000 library files plus
#   every project index to produce byte-identical symbols.
#
#   The same warning as those entries, and it is not boilerplate: the failure
#   mode of getting this wrong is SILENT. If a future edit to this file ever
#   influences what is indexed, the version MUST move.
#
#   Pinned by tests\autotest\run_index_freshness.ps1.

